From: ReadyNAS NG <readynasng@build>
Subject: [PATCH 26/27] ksmbd: zero-copy reads from the page cache

SMB2 READ zeroed a buffer (kvzalloc) of up to 4 MiB, copied the file into
it (kernel_read) and copied it again into the socket buffers
(kernel_sendmsg): three passes over every byte, which saturated the 280 MHz
CPU of the ReadyNAS at 5-7 MiB/s.

A read now takes the page cache pages through splice
(splice_direct_to_actor, as nfsd does), keeps a reference to them in the
work until it is freed, and points the response kvecs straight at them
(no highmem on this platform); the TCP transport sends runs of page cache
kvecs with MSG_SPLICE_PAGES, so the socket buffers reference the pages
(with a scatter-gather NIC; otherwise TCP copies as before). Signing
covers the header and every data kvec (read_iov_n), and works unchanged
since the data stays addressable.

Not used (the copying path below): SMB 3 encryption (it encrypts in
place), compound requests (padding after the data), RDMA, streams, reads
under one page, files without splice_read. The copying path no longer
zeroes its buffer, only the padding bytes after the data.

Run-time switch: /sys/module/ksmbd/parameters/zerocopy_read (default 1).
Measured on the NAS (500 MB over Wi-Fi, files compared byte for byte):
reads 17-20 MiB/s instead of 5-6, system time / 10, also when signed.
